export const G1={title:'The Genetics event, the cell cycle and mitosis',hook:'Every one of the trillions of cells in your body came from a single fertilized egg, copied with remarkable accuracy. Genetics begins with how cells copy their DNA and divide—and it is the first piece of the 20% “reproduction, mitosis and meiosis” portion of HOSA’s new Genetics test.',objectives:['Describe the structure of the Genetics event, its test plan and its skills.','Explain the phases of the cell cycle and where DNA is replicated.','Describe the stages of mitosis and cytokinesis.','Explain how checkpoints control the cell cycle and how their failure leads to cancer.'],sections:[
['The Genetics event',[
'Genetics is **new for 2026–27** and sponsored by **Carolina Biological Supply Company**, whose *Carolina Genetics: A Guidebook for the HOSA Genetics Written Test* is the official reference. **Round One** is a **50-question test in 60 minutes** (no time announcements at ILC) covering **reproduction, mitosis and meiosis (20%)**, **classical genetics (20%)**, **chromosomal inheritance (10%)**, **experimental design and model organisms (15%)**, **molecular genetics (15%)**, **population genetics (10%)** and **modern applications and careers (10%)**. **Round Two** is a scenario-based skills assessment: **Skill I** DNA extraction (20 minutes), **II-A** bacterial conjugation and **II-B** bacterial transduction (20 each), **III-A** a test cross with *Drosophila* and **III-B** with **Wisconsin Fast Plants** (25 each), **IV** genetic cross results interpretation (15) and **V** validating a mode of inheritance with a **chi-square test** (15).',
'Competitors bring photo ID, two #2 pencils, **safety glasses or goggles**, a **disposable gown**, **non-latex gloves**, a **full-face shield** (or equivalent) and **disposable masks**. Every lab skill begins with entering the testing area in **closed-toed shoes** and donning PPE, and ends with cleaning the work surface with disinfectant and removing PPE. Competitors need **70% on the combined skills** to be recognized; the final rank adds test and skill scores, with ties broken by the test score.',
match('Match the Round Two skill to its organism or tool',[['Skill I','DNA extraction from a fruit such as strawberries'],['Skill II-A','Bacterial conjugation with E. coli strains'],['Skill II-B','Bacterial transduction with a bacteriophage and serial dilutions'],['Skill III-A','Test cross with Drosophila'],['Skill III-B','Test cross with Wisconsin Fast Plants'],['Skill V','Chi-square test of a mode of inheritance']])]],
['Chromosomes and the cell cycle',[
'DNA in eukaryotic cells is packaged with proteins into **chromatin**, which condenses into **chromosomes** before division. A replicated chromosome has two identical **sister chromatids** joined at the **centromere**. Human body (**somatic**) cells are **diploid (2n = 46)**, with 23 pairs of **homologous chromosomes**—one of each pair from each parent—while gametes are **haploid (n = 23)**. The **cell cycle** has **interphase** and the **mitotic (M) phase**. Interphase includes **G1** (growth and normal functions), **S** (synthesis, when **DNA is replicated**) and **G2** (more growth and preparation for division). Some cells leave the cycle and enter **G0**, a non-dividing state—mature nerve cells, for example.',
order('Arrange the cell cycle in order',['G1: cell grows and carries out normal functions','S: DNA is replicated','G2: cell prepares for division','Mitosis: the nucleus divides','Cytokinesis: the cytoplasm divides']),
chk('During which phase of the cell cycle is DNA replicated?',['S','G1','G2','M'],'S stands for synthesis—a HOSA sample question.')]],
['Mitosis and cytokinesis',[
'**Mitosis** divides the nucleus so each daughter cell receives an identical, complete set of chromosomes. In **prophase**, chromosomes condense, the nuclear envelope breaks down and the **spindle** forms from centrosomes. (Some textbooks separate **prometaphase**, when spindle fibers attach to the **kinetochores** at centromeres.) In **metaphase**, chromosomes line up along the **metaphase plate** in the middle of the cell. In **anaphase**, sister chromatids separate and move to opposite poles. In **telophase**, chromosomes decondense and nuclear envelopes re-form. **Cytokinesis** then splits the cytoplasm—by a **cleavage furrow** in animal cells and a **cell plate** in plant cells—producing **two genetically identical diploid daughter cells**. Mitosis allows growth, tissue repair and **asexual reproduction**.',
flip('Phases of mitosis',[['Prophase','Chromosomes condense; spindle forms; nuclear envelope breaks down'],['Metaphase','Chromosomes line up at the cell’s middle'],['Anaphase','Sister chromatids pull apart to opposite poles'],['Telophase','Nuclear envelopes re-form; chromosomes relax'],['Cytokinesis','Cytoplasm divides; cleavage furrow or cell plate'],['Result','Two identical diploid cells']])]],
['Controlling the cycle and cancer',[
'The cell cycle is regulated by **checkpoints** that check for problems before the cell moves on. The **G1 checkpoint** checks cell size, nutrients, growth signals and DNA damage; the **G2 checkpoint** checks that DNA replication is complete and accurate; the **M (spindle) checkpoint** checks that every chromosome is attached to the spindle before anaphase. **Cyclins** and **cyclin-dependent kinases (CDKs)** drive the cycle forward. **Tumor suppressor genes** such as ***p53*** (which halts the cycle to repair DNA or triggers **apoptosis**, programmed cell death) act as brakes, while **proto-oncogenes** promote division; mutated proto-oncogenes become **oncogenes** that act like a stuck accelerator. **Cancer** results when mutations accumulate in these genes and cells divide uncontrollably, invade tissues and may spread (**metastasis**).',
sort('Brake or accelerator?',['Slows or stops division (tumor suppressor role)','Promotes division (proto-oncogene/oncogene role)'],[['p53 halting the cycle after DNA damage',0],['Triggering apoptosis in a damaged cell',0],['A growth-factor signal gene stuck “on”',1],['Cyclin–CDK complexes pushing the cell forward',1],['A checkpoint protein detecting unattached chromosomes',0]])]],
['Asexual reproduction and why it matters in genetics',[
'**Asexual reproduction** produces offspring genetically identical to the parent (barring mutation). Bacteria divide by **binary fission**; yeast can reproduce by **budding**; many plants reproduce by runners or cuttings. Because offspring are clones, asexual reproduction is fast and efficient but produces little genetic variation, which is one reason bacteria exchange genes by **transformation, conjugation and transduction**—the processes in Skill II. Sexual reproduction, covered next, combines genes from two parents and generates variation through meiosis and fertilization. Knowing which type of reproduction a model organism uses helps explain why scientists choose it for an experiment.',
kase('Interpreting a cell count',{a:{text:'A researcher counts 100 cells in a root tip: 85 in interphase, 8 in prophase, 3 in metaphase, 2 in anaphase and 2 in telophase. What can she conclude?',choices:[['Most of the cell cycle is spent in interphase','b','Correct. The proportion of cells in a phase reflects the time spent in it.',true],['The cells are not dividing','b','Cells in mitotic phases show division is occurring.',false]]},b:{text:'In a tumor sample, 40% of cells are in mitosis. What does this suggest?',choices:[['Cells are dividing far more often than normal, consistent with loss of cycle control','c','Correct. Rapid division is a hallmark of cancer.',true],['The tumor is healthy tissue','c','The mitotic index is abnormally high.',false]]},c:{text:'The researcher calculates a mitotic index (cells in mitosis ÷ total cells) to compare tissues. Data are invented for practice.',end:true}})]],
["Observing mitosis under the microscope",["Students often observe mitosis in **onion root tips** or **whitefish blastula** slides, where cells divide rapidly. Root tips are fixed, softened in acid, stained with a DNA stain so chromosomes appear dark, squashed into a thin layer and viewed at low power to find the region of dividing cells, then at high power (400×) to identify phases. **Interphase** cells show a distinct nucleus and no visible chromosomes; **prophase** shows thick, tangled chromosomes; **metaphase** a line of chromosomes across the middle; **anaphase** two groups moving apart; and **telophase** two clusters at opposite ends with a forming cell plate. Counting cells in each phase lets you estimate how long each phase lasts relative to the whole cycle, because phases that take longer contain more cells at any moment.","The **mitotic index** = (number of cells in mitosis ÷ total cells counted) × 100. Tissues that grow quickly—such as root tips, embryos, skin and tumors—have high mitotic indexes. Scientists use this measure to compare growth under different conditions, such as the effect of a chemical on cell division, which is also a simple example of experimental design.",{"t":"example","title":"Calculating time in each phase","prompt":"In a root tip, 200 cells are counted: 160 interphase, 20 prophase, 8 metaphase, 6 anaphase and 6 telophase. If the cell cycle lasts 24 hours, about how long is prophase? What is the mitotic index?","steps":["Fraction in prophase = 20 ÷ 200 = 0.10.","Time in prophase ≈ 0.10 × 24 h = 2.4 hours.","Cells in mitosis = 20 + 8 + 6 + 6 = 40.","Mitotic index = 40 ÷ 200 × 100 = 20%."],"answer":"Prophase lasts about 2.4 hours, and the mitotic index is 20%. (Counts are invented for practice.)"}]]
],takeaways:['Test plan: reproduction/mitosis/meiosis 20%, classical genetics 20%, chromosomal 10%, experimental design 15%, molecular 15%, population 10%, applications/careers 10%.','Cell cycle: G1, S (DNA replication), G2, then mitosis (prophase, metaphase, anaphase, telophase) and cytokinesis, producing two identical diploid cells.','Checkpoints, cyclins/CDKs, tumor suppressors (p53) and proto-oncogenes regulate division; their mutation can cause cancer.'],terms:[['Sister chromatids','Identical copies of a replicated chromosome joined at the centromere.'],['Diploid','Having two sets of chromosomes (2n).'],['Apoptosis','Programmed cell death.']],mcqs:[q('During which phase of the cell cycle is DNA replicated?','S','G1','G2','M','A HOSA sample question.'),q('In which phase do chromosomes line up at the cell’s center?','Metaphase','Prophase','Anaphase','Telophase','They align on the metaphase plate.'),q('Sister chromatids separate during:','Anaphase','Metaphase','Interphase','G1','They move to opposite poles.'),q('Mitosis produces:','Two genetically identical diploid cells','Four haploid cells','Two haploid gametes','One cell','It supports growth and repair.'),q('Human somatic cells have how many chromosomes?','46','23','92','44','23 pairs.'),q('Which gene acts as a “brake” on cell division?','p53, a tumor suppressor','An oncogene','A cyclin','A growth factor','It can trigger apoptosis.'),q('Plant cells complete cytokinesis by forming a:','Cell plate','Cleavage furrow','Spindle','Centromere','Animal cells form a cleavage furrow.'),q('Which organization sponsors the Genetics event?','Carolina Biological Supply Company','AAFP','American Dental Association','NIH','Its guidebook is the official reference.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe the cell cycle and mitosis, and explain how failures of cell cycle control can lead to cancer.',rubric:[['Interphase phases',['g1','s phase','synthesis','replicat','g2']],['Mitosis stages',['prophase','metaphase','anaphase','telophase','cytokinesis']],['Outcome',['identical','diploid','two','daughter']],['Control and cancer',['checkpoint','p53','tumor suppressor','oncogene','cyclin','uncontrolled']]],model:'The cell cycle has interphase—G1, when the cell grows and functions; S, when DNA is replicated so each chromosome has two sister chromatids; and G2, when the cell prepares to divide—followed by mitosis and cytokinesis. In prophase, chromosomes condense, the spindle forms and the nuclear envelope breaks down; in metaphase, chromosomes line up at the center; in anaphase, sister chromatids separate to opposite poles; and in telophase, nuclear envelopes re-form. Cytokinesis divides the cytoplasm, producing two genetically identical diploid daughter cells. Checkpoints at G1, G2 and the spindle check for size, DNA damage, complete replication and proper attachment, driven by cyclins and CDKs. Tumor suppressor genes such as p53 stop the cycle or trigger apoptosis when DNA is damaged, while proto-oncogenes promote division. When mutations inactivate tumor suppressors or turn proto-oncogenes into oncogenes, cells ignore these controls, divide uncontrollably and can form tumors that invade and metastasize—cancer.'},sources:[RULES,CAROLINA,OS_CYCLE,OS_CONTROL]};

export const G2={title:'Meiosis and sexual reproduction',hook:'Two siblings with the same parents can look very different. Meiosis is the reason: it halves the chromosome number and shuffles genes so that each egg and sperm is genetically unique. Understanding meiosis explains Mendel’s laws, inherited disorders and genetic variation.',objectives:['Describe the stages of meiosis I and meiosis II.','Explain how crossing over and independent assortment create variation.','Compare mitosis and meiosis.','Describe gametogenesis, fertilization and nondisjunction.'],sections:[
['Why meiosis?',[
'Sexual reproduction combines a haploid **egg** and a haploid **sperm** at **fertilization** to form a diploid **zygote**. If gametes were diploid, the chromosome number would double every generation. **Meiosis** prevents this: one diploid cell undergoes **one round of DNA replication followed by two divisions**, producing **four haploid cells**, each genetically different. In humans, meiosis takes place in the **ovaries** and **testes**; in flowering plants such as Wisconsin Fast Plants, it occurs in the **anthers** (producing pollen) and **ovules** (producing eggs).']],
['Meiosis I: separating homologs',[
'After DNA replication in interphase, **meiosis I** separates **homologous chromosomes**. In **prophase I**, homologs pair up along their length (**synapsis**), forming **tetrads** (bivalents), and nonsister chromatids exchange segments in **crossing over** at points called **chiasmata**, creating new combinations of alleles. In **metaphase I**, homologous **pairs** line up at the metaphase plate, each pair oriented randomly—**independent assortment**. In **anaphase I**, homologs separate to opposite poles while sister chromatids stay together. In **telophase I** and cytokinesis, two **haploid** cells form, each chromosome still made of two chromatids. Meiosis I is called the **reduction division**.',
order('Events of meiosis I in order',['DNA replicates during interphase','Prophase I: homologs pair and cross over','Metaphase I: homologous pairs line up at the plate','Anaphase I: homologs separate','Telophase I: two haploid cells form'])]],
['Meiosis II: separating sister chromatids',[
'**Meiosis II** resembles mitosis but starts with haploid cells and no further DNA replication. In **prophase II** the spindle forms again; in **metaphase II** chromosomes line up individually; in **anaphase II** **sister chromatids separate**; and in **telophase II** with cytokinesis, the result is **four haploid daughter cells**, each with one chromatid per chromosome. Because of crossing over and independent assortment, the four cells are genetically different from one another and from the parent cell. In humans, independent assortment alone allows 2²³—about 8.4 million—combinations of chromosomes per gamete, before crossing over adds even more.',
sort('Mitosis, meiosis or both?',['Mitosis only','Meiosis only','Both'],[['Produces two identical diploid cells',0],['Produces four haploid cells',1],['Crossing over between homologs',1],['Homologous pairs line up together at metaphase I',1],['Sister chromatids separate at some point',2],['Preceded by DNA replication',2],['Used for growth and tissue repair',0]])]],
['Gametogenesis and fertilization',[
'**Spermatogenesis** in the testes produces **four functional sperm** from each primary spermatocyte, continuously from puberty. **Oogenesis** in the ovaries produces **one egg** and up to three small **polar bodies** from each primary oocyte, because cytoplasm divides unequally to give the egg most of the nutrients; oogenesis begins before birth, pauses in prophase I and resumes at ovulation, with meiosis II completed only if fertilization occurs. At fertilization, the haploid sperm and egg nuclei combine to restore the diploid number. The sex of human offspring is determined by the sperm: an egg always carries an X chromosome, and a sperm carries either X or Y.',
match('Match the term to its description',[['Synapsis','Pairing of homologous chromosomes'],['Chiasma','Point where crossing over occurs'],['Polar body','Small cell produced during oogenesis'],['Zygote','Diploid cell formed by fertilization'],['Tetrad','Paired homologs with four chromatids'],['Reduction division','Meiosis I, which halves the chromosome number']])]],
['When meiosis goes wrong: nondisjunction',[
'**Nondisjunction** is the failure of chromosomes to separate correctly—homologs in anaphase I or sister chromatids in anaphase II. It produces gametes with an extra or missing chromosome; after fertilization, the zygote has **aneuploidy**, such as **trisomy** (three copies) or **monosomy** (one copy). Examples include **Down syndrome (trisomy 21)**, **Turner syndrome (45,X)** and **Klinefelter syndrome (47,XXY)**. The risk of nondisjunction increases with maternal age. Karyotypes, covered in the chromosomal inheritance lesson, are used to detect these changes.',
kase('Tracing nondisjunction',{a:{text:'During meiosis I, chromosome 21 homologs fail to separate. What gametes result?',choices:[['Two gametes with an extra chromosome 21 and two missing chromosome 21','b','Correct. Both homologs move to one side in meiosis I.',true],['Four normal gametes','b','Nondisjunction produces abnormal chromosome numbers.',false]]},b:{text:'A gamete with an extra chromosome 21 is fertilized by a normal gamete. What is the zygote’s chromosome count?',choices:[['47, trisomy 21 (Down syndrome)','c','Correct. 24 + 23 = 47.',true],['46, normal','c','The extra chromosome makes 47.',false]]},c:{text:'A karyotype of the child would show three copies of chromosome 21.',end:true}})]],
["Sexual reproduction in flowering plants",["Plants also use meiosis, which matters for Wisconsin Fast Plants. A flower’s **male** parts are the **stamens**, each with a stalk (**filament**) and an **anther**, where meiosis produces **pollen grains**. The **female** part is the **pistil** (carpel), with a sticky **stigma** that catches pollen, a **style** and an **ovary** containing **ovules**, where meiosis produces the egg. In **pollination**, pollen moves from an anther to a stigma—by insects, wind or, in the lab, a **pollination wand**. A **pollen tube** grows down the style, and in flowering plants **double fertilization** occurs: one sperm fertilizes the egg to form a diploid **zygote** (the embryo), and another joins two polar nuclei to form triploid **endosperm**, the seed’s food supply. The ovule becomes the **seed** and the ovary becomes the **fruit** or seed pod.","Plant life cycles show **alternation of generations** between a diploid **sporophyte** (the visible plant in flowering plants) and tiny haploid **gametophytes** (the pollen grain and the embryo sac). Because Fast Plants are **self-incompatible**, they cannot fertilize themselves, so every seed in Skill III-B comes from a deliberate cross between two chosen parents—exactly what a genetics experiment needs.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Match the flower part to its role","pairs":[["Anther","Produces pollen by meiosis"],["Filament","Stalk that holds the anther"],["Stigma","Sticky tip that receives pollen"],["Style","Tube the pollen tube grows through"],["Ovary","Contains ovules and becomes the seed pod"],["Ovule","Contains the egg and becomes the seed"]]},{"t":"check","q":"Which flower structure should receive the pollen in a Fast Plants cross?","options":["The stigma of the pistil","The anther","The petal","The filament"],"why":"Pollen lands on the stigma, the top of the pistil."}]]
],takeaways:['Meiosis: one DNA replication and two divisions produce four genetically different haploid cells.','Meiosis I separates homologs (crossing over in prophase I, independent assortment in metaphase I); meiosis II separates sister chromatids.','Spermatogenesis yields four sperm; oogenesis one egg and polar bodies; nondisjunction causes trisomy 21, Turner and Klinefelter syndromes.'],terms:[['Crossing over','Exchange of segments between nonsister chromatids of homologs.'],['Independent assortment','Random orientation of homologous pairs at metaphase I.'],['Nondisjunction','Failure of chromosomes to separate during meiosis.']],mcqs:[q('Meiosis produces:','Four genetically different haploid cells','Two identical diploid cells','Four identical diploid cells','One haploid cell','Variation arises from crossing over and assortment.'),q('Crossing over occurs during:','Prophase I','Metaphase II','Anaphase II','Telophase I','Homologs are paired in synapsis.'),q('Homologous chromosomes separate in:','Anaphase I','Anaphase II','Mitotic anaphase','Prophase II','Meiosis I is the reduction division.'),q('Independent assortment occurs because of:','Random orientation of homologous pairs at metaphase I','DNA replication','Cytokinesis','Fertilization only','Each pair aligns independently.'),q('Oogenesis produces from each primary oocyte:','One egg and polar bodies','Four eggs','Four sperm','Two eggs','Cytoplasm divides unequally.'),q('Trisomy 21 results from:','Nondisjunction','Crossing over','Normal meiosis','Mitosis','An extra chromosome 21.'),q('Which parent’s gamete determines the sex of a human child?','The father’s sperm (X or Y)','The mother’s egg','Both equally','Neither','Eggs always carry X.'),q('Human gametes contain how many chromosomes?','23','46','92','22','They are haploid.')],frq:{prompt:'Compare mitosis and meiosis, and explain the two processes in meiosis that create genetic variation.',rubric:[['Mitosis outcome and purpose',['two','identical','diploid','growth','repair']],['Meiosis outcome',['four','haploid','gamete','two divisions']],['Crossing over',['crossing over','prophase i','homolog','chiasma','exchange']],['Independent assortment',['independent assortment','metaphase i','random','orientation']]],model:'Mitosis is one division following DNA replication that produces two genetically identical diploid cells for growth, tissue repair and asexual reproduction. Meiosis has one DNA replication followed by two divisions: meiosis I separates homologous chromosomes and meiosis II separates sister chromatids, producing four haploid gametes that are genetically different. Variation arises first from crossing over in prophase I, when paired homologs exchange segments at chiasmata, creating chromosomes with new combinations of alleles. Second, during metaphase I, each homologous pair lines up independently of the others, so the maternal and paternal chromosomes are sorted randomly into gametes—independent assortment, which alone gives about 8 million combinations in humans. Random fertilization then adds even more variation.'},sources:[RULES,CAROLINA,OS_MEIOSIS,OS_DISORD]};

export const G3={title:'Mendelian genetics and Punnett squares',hook:'In a monastery garden in the 1850s and 1860s, Gregor Mendel crossed thousands of pea plants and counted their offspring. His ratios—3:1 and 9:3:3:1—revealed that traits are passed on as discrete units we now call genes. Classical genetics makes up 20% of the test and underlies Skills IV and V.',objectives:['Use the vocabulary of classical genetics correctly.','State Mendel’s laws of segregation and independent assortment.','Set up and solve monohybrid, test and dihybrid crosses.','Apply the product and sum rules of probability to genetic problems.'],sections:[
['Mendel and his peas',[
'**Gregor Mendel** chose garden peas because they have easily distinguished traits, can **self-pollinate** or be **cross-pollinated** and produce many offspring. He began with **true-breeding** (homozygous) parents—the **P generation**—and crossed them to produce the **F1 generation**, then allowed F1 plants to self-fertilize to produce the **F2**. Crossing purple-flowered and white-flowered plants gave all purple F1 plants, but white flowers reappeared in about **one-quarter** of the F2: a **3:1** ratio. Mendel concluded that each plant carries two “factors” (alleles) for each trait, one of which may mask the other.',
match('Match the term to its definition',[['Gene','A segment of DNA that codes for a trait'],['Allele','An alternative form of a gene'],['Genotype','The alleles an organism has (e.g., Pp)'],['Phenotype','The observable trait (e.g., purple flowers)'],['Homozygous','Two identical alleles (PP or pp)'],['Heterozygous','Two different alleles (Pp)']])]],
['Mendel’s laws',[
'The **law of segregation** states that the two alleles for a gene separate during gamete formation, so each gamete receives only one allele. This happens because homologous chromosomes separate in meiosis I. The **law of independent assortment** states that alleles of **different genes** are sorted into gametes independently of one another, because homologous pairs orient randomly at metaphase I—true for genes on **different chromosomes** or far apart on the same chromosome. A **dominant** allele (capital letter, P) is expressed in the heterozygote; a **recessive** allele (lowercase, p) is expressed only when homozygous.',
chk('Which event in meiosis explains the law of segregation?',['Separation of homologous chromosomes in anaphase I','DNA replication','Cytokinesis in mitosis','Crossing over only'],'Each gamete receives one allele of each gene.')]],
['Monohybrid crosses and the test cross',[
'A **Punnett square** shows the possible combinations of gametes. Crossing two heterozygotes (**Pp × Pp**) gives genotypes **1 PP : 2 Pp : 1 pp** and phenotypes **3 dominant : 1 recessive**. A **test cross** determines the genotype of an individual with the dominant phenotype by crossing it with a **homozygous recessive** individual: if all offspring show the dominant phenotype, the unknown parent is probably **homozygous dominant**; if about half show the recessive phenotype, it is **heterozygous** (a **1:1** ratio). Skill III asks competitors to set up test crosses with *Drosophila* or Wisconsin Fast Plants, and Skill IV to solve monohybrid Punnett squares.',
{t:'w',kind:'punnett',mode:'mono',title:'Build a monohybrid cross',prompt:'Try Aa × Aa, then a test cross (Aa × aa). Predict the share with the dominant trait before building the square.'},
{t:'example',title:'Reading a test cross',prompt:'A purple-flowered pea plant of unknown genotype is crossed with a white-flowered plant (pp). The offspring are 48 purple and 52 white. What is the unknown genotype?',steps:['The white parent can only give p gametes.','White offspring (pp) must have received a p from the unknown parent.','About a 1:1 ratio of purple to white is expected from Pp × pp.'],answer:'The unknown parent is heterozygous, Pp. (Counts are invented for practice.)'}]],
['Dihybrid crosses',[
'A **dihybrid cross** follows two genes at once. Mendel crossed plants heterozygous for seed color (Yy, yellow dominant) and seed shape (Rr, round dominant): **YyRr × YyRr**. Each parent makes four kinds of gametes—**YR, Yr, yR and yr**—in equal proportions by independent assortment, giving a 4 × 4 Punnett square with 16 boxes and a phenotypic ratio of **9 yellow round : 3 yellow wrinkled : 3 green round : 1 green wrinkled**. The 9:3:3:1 ratio is the signature of two independently assorting genes with complete dominance; a dihybrid **test cross** (YyRr × yyrr) gives **1:1:1:1**. Skill IV includes solving a dihybrid Punnett square, and Skill V asks for an expected dihybrid cross in a chi-square test.',
{t:'w',kind:'punnett',mode:'dihybrid',title:'Build a dihybrid cross',prompt:'Cross two double heterozygotes and confirm the 9:3:3:1 phenotypic ratio.'}]],
['Probability in genetics',[
'Punnett squares apply probability. The **product rule** says the probability of two independent events both occurring is the product of their probabilities: the chance of a **yyrr** offspring from YyRr × YyRr is ¼ (yy) × ¼ (rr) = **1/16**. The **sum rule** says the probability of either of two mutually exclusive events is the sum: the chance of a heterozygote from Pp × Pp is ¼ (Pp from P sperm and p egg) + ¼ (pP) = **½**. These rules are faster than large Punnett squares for crosses with three or more genes—for example, the chance of AaBbCc from AaBbCc × AaBbCc is ½ × ½ × ½ = 1/8.',
match('Match the cross to its expected ratio',[['Pp × Pp (phenotypes)','3:1'],['Pp × Pp (genotypes)','1:2:1'],['Pp × pp (test cross)','1:1'],['YyRr × YyRr (phenotypes)','9:3:3:1'],['YyRr × yyrr','1:1:1:1'],['PP × pp','All dominant phenotype']]),
kase('Probability practice',{a:{text:'Two parents are both Aa for a recessive condition. What is the chance their child is affected?',choices:[['1/4','b','Correct. Only aa is affected.',true],['1/2','b','That is the chance of being a carrier (Aa).',false]]},b:{text:'What is the chance that two children in a row are both affected?',choices:[['1/16, using the product rule','c','Correct. ¼ × ¼ = 1/16; each birth is independent.',true],['1/8','c','Multiply the independent probabilities.',false]]},c:{text:'Genetic counselors use these rules to explain recurrence risks to families.',end:true}})]],
["Why Mendel succeeded—and the forked-line method",["Mendel’s success came from **experimental design**. He used **true-breeding** lines so parents’ genotypes were known; studied **seven traits with two clear forms** each; controlled pollination by removing anthers and transferring pollen by hand; counted **large numbers** of offspring (thousands of plants); recorded results **quantitatively** and looked for **ratios**; and performed **reciprocal crosses** and **test crosses** to check his model. Modern geneticists still follow these principles, and statistical tests such as chi-square formalize Mendel’s question: do the observed counts fit the predicted ratio?","For crosses with several genes, the **forked-line (branch) method** is faster than a large Punnett square. Treat each gene separately, list its outcomes and probabilities, then multiply along each branch. For **AaBb × AaBb**, gene A gives ¾ A_ and ¼ aa; gene B gives ¾ B_ and ¼ bb. Branches: A_B_ = ¾ × ¾ = 9/16; A_bb = ¾ × ¼ = 3/16; aaB_ = 3/16; aabb = 1/16—the familiar 9:3:3:1.",{"t":"example","title":"A three-gene probability","prompt":"In the cross AaBbCc × AaBbCc (all genes unlinked, complete dominance), what fraction of offspring show all three dominant phenotypes? What fraction are aabbcc?","steps":["Each gene gives a ¾ chance of the dominant phenotype.","All three dominant: ¾ × ¾ × ¾ = 27/64.","Each gene gives a ¼ chance of homozygous recessive.","aabbcc: ¼ × ¼ × ¼ = 1/64."],"answer":"27/64 show all three dominant phenotypes, and 1/64 are aabbcc."}]]
],takeaways:['Mendel’s monohybrid F2 ratio is 3:1; segregation separates alleles; independent assortment sorts genes on different chromosomes independently.','A test cross with a homozygous recessive reveals an unknown genotype: all dominant (homozygous) or 1:1 (heterozygous).','Dihybrid YyRr × YyRr gives 9:3:3:1; use the product rule (and) and sum rule (or) for probabilities.'],terms:[['Test cross','Cross with a homozygous recessive to reveal an unknown genotype.'],['True-breeding','Always producing offspring with the same trait when self-fertilized.'],['F1 generation','First filial generation from crossing the parental generation.']],mcqs:[q('A cross of two heterozygotes (Pp × Pp) gives which phenotypic ratio?','3:1','1:1','9:3:3:1','1:2:1','The genotypic ratio is 1:2:1.'),q('A test cross uses an individual that is:','Homozygous recessive','Homozygous dominant','Heterozygous','Any phenotype','It reveals the unknown genotype.'),q('If a test cross gives a 1:1 ratio, the unknown parent is:','Heterozygous','Homozygous dominant','Homozygous recessive','Polyploid','Half the offspring receive the recessive allele.'),q('YyRr × YyRr produces which phenotypic ratio?','9:3:3:1','3:1','1:1:1:1','1:2:1','Two independently assorting genes.'),q('The probability of yyrr from YyRr × YyRr is:','1/16','1/4','1/8','9/16','¼ × ¼.'),q('The law of independent assortment applies to genes that are:','On different chromosomes (or far apart)','Tightly linked','Identical','On the Y chromosome only','Linked genes do not assort independently.'),q('An organism with genotype Pp is:','Heterozygous','Homozygous dominant','Homozygous recessive','Haploid','Two different alleles.'),q('How many types of gametes does a YyRr individual make?','Four','Two','One','Sixteen','YR, Yr, yR and yr.')],frq:{prompt:'A tall plant (T) with purple flowers (P) of unknown genotype is crossed with a short, white-flowered plant (ttpp). Explain how you would use the offspring to determine the unknown genotype, and predict the ratio if the unknown plant is TtPp.',rubric:[['Test cross concept',['test cross','homozygous recessive','ttpp','unknown']],['Gametes of TtPp',['tp','tp','tp','four','gamete']],['Expected ratio',['1:1:1:1','tall purple','short white','equal']],['Interpretation of other outcomes',['all tall purple','homozygous','ttpp','ttpp','independent']]],model:'This is a dihybrid test cross: the short, white plant is homozygous recessive (ttpp) and can only produce tp gametes, so every offspring’s phenotype reveals which alleles the unknown parent contributed. If the unknown plant is TtPp, independent assortment produces four gamete types—TP, Tp, tP and tp—in equal proportions, giving offspring TtPp (tall purple), Ttpp (tall white), ttPp (short purple) and ttpp (short white) in a 1:1:1:1 ratio. If all offspring are tall and purple, the unknown is probably TTPP; if they are half tall purple and half tall white, it is TTPp; if half tall purple and half short purple, it is TtPP. Large numbers of offspring and a chi-square test would confirm whether the observed counts fit the predicted ratio, and a large excess of parental types would suggest the genes are linked rather than assorting independently.'},sources:[RULES,CAROLINA,OS_MENDEL,OS_LAWS]};

export const G4={title:'Beyond Mendel: non-Mendelian inheritance and pedigrees',hook:'Pink snapdragons from red and white parents, AB blood type and skin color with many shades all break Mendel’s simple dominant–recessive pattern. Skill IV asks you to recognize these patterns, solve a blood-typing cross and read a pedigree across generations.',objectives:['Explain incomplete dominance, codominance, multiple alleles, polygenic inheritance, epistasis and pleiotropy.','Solve human ABO blood-type crosses.','Read pedigrees and identify autosomal, X-linked, Y-linked and mitochondrial inheritance.','Assign genotypes to individuals in a pedigree.'],sections:[
['Patterns beyond simple dominance',[
'In **incomplete dominance**, the heterozygote has an **intermediate phenotype**: red (C^R C^R) × white (C^W C^W) snapdragons give **pink** (C^R C^W) offspring, and an F2 ratio of **1 red : 2 pink : 1 white**—phenotypic and genotypic ratios match. In **codominance**, **both alleles are fully expressed**, such as the **AB blood type** or roan cattle with both red and white hairs. **Multiple alleles** means a gene has more than two alleles in the population, as in ABO blood types (I^A, I^B, i). **Polygenic inheritance** involves many genes adding together, producing continuous variation such as height and skin color, often a bell-shaped curve. **Epistasis** occurs when one gene masks or modifies another, as in Labrador coat color, where a second gene determines whether pigment is deposited. **Pleiotropy** is one gene affecting many traits, as in sickle cell disease. **Environment** also influences phenotype, such as temperature affecting coat color in Himalayan rabbits.',
match('Match the pattern to the example',[['Incomplete dominance','Pink snapdragons from red and white parents'],['Codominance','AB blood type'],['Multiple alleles','Three ABO alleles in the population'],['Polygenic inheritance','Continuous variation in human height'],['Epistasis','One gene controls whether coat pigment is deposited'],['Pleiotropy','One gene affects many organs in sickle cell disease']])]],
['Blood typing crosses',[
'The ABO gene has three alleles: **I^A** and **I^B** are **codominant** to each other and both are **dominant** to **i**. Blood types: **A** (I^A I^A or I^A i), **B** (I^B I^B or I^B i), **AB** (I^A I^B) and **O** (ii). The **Rh factor** is inherited separately: Rh-positive (D) is dominant to Rh-negative (d). Example: a type A parent (I^A i) and a type B parent (I^B i) can have children with **all four blood types**, each with a ¼ chance. Blood-type crosses can exclude, but never prove, a parentage relationship. Skill IV includes setting up and solving a human blood-typing Punnett square.',
{t:'example',title:'Solve a blood-type cross',prompt:'A mother with type AB blood and a father with type O blood have a child. Which blood types are possible?',steps:['Mother I^A I^B makes I^A and I^B gametes.','Father ii makes only i gametes.','Combine: I^A i (type A) and I^B i (type B).'],answer:'Type A or type B, each with a ½ chance; types AB and O are not possible.'},
{t:'w',kind:'abotype',title:'Read blood types from agglutination',prompt:'Connect phenotype to genotype: which antigens are present for each blood type?'}]],
['Reading pedigrees',[
'A **pedigree** is a family chart. **Squares** represent males and **circles** females; **shaded** symbols are affected individuals, and half-shaded or dotted symbols may show carriers. A horizontal line between a male and female shows mating; a vertical line leads to their offspring, listed in birth order. Generations are labeled with **Roman numerals** (I, II, III) and individuals with numbers. To read a pedigree, ask: Does the trait **skip generations**? (Recessive traits often do.) Do **affected children have unaffected parents**? (Recessive.) Is it more common in **males**? (X-linked recessive.) Does an affected father pass it to **all daughters and no sons**? (X-linked dominant.) Does it pass from father to **all sons only**? (Y-linked.) Is it passed only through the **mother** to all her children? (Mitochondrial.)',
sort('Which pattern best fits?',['Autosomal recessive','Autosomal dominant','X-linked recessive','Mitochondrial'],[['Two unaffected parents have an affected daughter',0],['Every affected person has an affected parent; males and females equally',1],['Mostly affected males whose mothers are carriers',2],['An affected mother passes the trait to all of her children; an affected father to none',3],['The trait skips a generation and appears in cousins from a consanguineous mating',0],['An affected father passes it to about half his sons and half his daughters',1]])]],
['Assigning genotypes',[
'Skill IV asks competitors to identify parental and filial generations, assign **phenotypes and genotype symbols** to every individual and identify the mode of inheritance. Work systematically: first decide whether the trait is dominant or recessive and autosomal or sex-linked; then label every affected individual (aa for autosomal recessive, X^aY or X^aX^a for X-linked recessive); then work outward—unaffected parents of an affected child with a recessive trait must both be **carriers** (Aa); unaffected individuals with an affected parent must carry at least one recessive allele. Use “A_” when the second allele cannot be determined. Check that every assigned genotype is consistent with both parents and children.',
kase('Solving a pedigree',{a:{text:'Generation I: an unaffected male and unaffected female. Generation II: they have three children—an affected son, an unaffected son and an unaffected daughter. The trait is rare. What is the most likely pattern?',choices:[['Recessive—affected child of unaffected parents','b','Correct. Both parents must carry the allele (or the mother, if X-linked).',true],['Dominant','b','A dominant trait would appear in at least one parent.',false]]},b:{text:'Later, an affected daughter is born to an affected father and a carrier mother. Which pattern does this still allow?',choices:[['Both autosomal recessive and X-linked recessive remain possible','c','Correct. An affected daughter can occur in either pattern with these parents; more data are needed.',true],['Only Y-linked','c','Y-linked traits never affect females.',false]]},c:{text:'You would look for male-to-male transmission (which rules out X-linkage) and the sex ratio of affected individuals to decide.',end:true}})]],
['Human genetic disorders',[
'Examples help recognize patterns on the test. **Autosomal recessive**: cystic fibrosis, sickle cell disease, Tay-Sachs disease and phenylketonuria (PKU). **Autosomal dominant**: Huntington disease, Marfan syndrome and achondroplasia. **X-linked recessive**: hemophilia A, Duchenne muscular dystrophy and red-green color blindness—far more common in males, who have only one X. **X-linked dominant**: hypophosphatemic rickets. **Y-linked**: some genes involved in male fertility. **Mitochondrial**: disorders such as Leber hereditary optic neuropathy, passed from mothers through the egg’s mitochondria. Carriers of recessive disorders are usually healthy but can pass the allele to children.',
flip('Name the inheritance pattern',[['Cystic fibrosis','Autosomal recessive'],['Huntington disease','Autosomal dominant'],['Hemophilia A','X-linked recessive'],['Duchenne muscular dystrophy','X-linked recessive'],['Leber hereditary optic neuropathy','Mitochondrial'],['Sickle cell disease','Autosomal recessive (codominant at the cellular level)']])]],
["More exceptions: lethal alleles, penetrance and sex-influenced traits",["Some alleles are **lethal** when homozygous. In mice, the yellow coat allele (A^Y) is dominant for color but lethal in the homozygous state, so a cross of two yellow mice gives a **2:1** ratio of yellow to non-yellow instead of 3:1—a classic chi-square puzzle. **Penetrance** is the percentage of individuals with a genotype who actually show the phenotype; **incomplete penetrance** means some carriers of a dominant allele are unaffected, which can make a dominant trait seem to skip a generation. **Expressivity** describes how strongly a trait is expressed, which can vary among affected individuals.","**Sex-influenced traits** are autosomal but expressed differently in males and females, such as pattern baldness, which is dominant in males and recessive in females because of hormones. **Sex-limited traits** appear in only one sex, such as milk production in cattle or beard growth. **Genomic imprinting** causes some genes to be expressed only from the mother’s or the father’s copy. Recognizing these patterns helps explain unexpected ratios and pedigrees.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Which concept explains the observation?","buckets":["Lethal allele","Incomplete penetrance","Sex-limited or sex-influenced"],"items":[["Two yellow mice produce 2 yellow : 1 non-yellow offspring",0],["A person with a dominant disease allele shows no symptoms",1],["Only female cattle produce milk",2],["Pattern baldness is more common in males",2],["Expected homozygotes never appear among offspring",0],["A dominant trait seems to skip a generation",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['Incomplete dominance blends (1:2:1), codominance expresses both (AB), and multiple alleles, polygenic traits, epistasis and pleiotropy extend Mendel.','ABO: I^A and I^B codominant, both dominant to i; type A × type B heterozygotes can produce all four types.','Pedigrees: squares male, circles female, shaded affected; recessive traits skip generations, X-linked recessive mostly affects males, mitochondrial passes from mothers.'],terms:[['Codominance','Both alleles fully expressed in the heterozygote.'],['Pedigree','Chart of a trait through generations of a family.'],['Carrier','A heterozygote who carries a recessive allele without showing the trait.']],mcqs:[q('Red × white snapdragons produce pink offspring. This is:','Incomplete dominance','Codominance','Epistasis','Sex linkage','The heterozygote is intermediate.'),q('A person with genotype I^A I^B has blood type:','AB','A','B','O','I^A and I^B are codominant.'),q('Parents with type A (I^A i) and type B (I^B i) can have children with:','A, B, AB or O blood','Only AB','Only A or B','Only O','Each has a ¼ chance.'),q('On a pedigree, a shaded circle represents:','An affected female','An unaffected male','An affected male','A carrier male','Circles are females.'),q('Which pattern often skips generations?','Recessive inheritance','Dominant inheritance','Y-linked','None','Carriers are unaffected.'),q('Hemophilia A is inherited as:','X-linked recessive','Autosomal dominant','Mitochondrial','Y-linked','It mostly affects males.'),q('Mitochondrial traits are passed:','From the mother to all her children','From the father to sons only','From either parent equally','Never','Mitochondria come from the egg.'),q('A trait controlled by many genes with continuous variation is:','Polygenic','Codominant','X-linked','Mitochondrial','Height is an example.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how you would determine the mode of inheritance from a three-generation pedigree, describing the clues for autosomal recessive, autosomal dominant, X-linked recessive and mitochondrial inheritance.',rubric:[['Pedigree conventions',['square','circle','shaded','generation','roman']],['Autosomal patterns',['skip','unaffected parents','carrier','every generation','dominant']],['X-linked recessive',['male','mother','carrier','x-linked']],['Mitochondrial and genotype assignment',['mitochondrial','mother','all children','genotype','aa']]],model:'I would first read the conventions—squares are males, circles females, shaded symbols affected, horizontal lines matings and Roman numerals generations. If two unaffected parents have an affected child and the trait skips generations, it is recessive; if males and females are affected equally and affected individuals can be daughters of unaffected fathers, it is likely autosomal recessive, and both unaffected parents of an affected child must be carriers (Aa). If the trait appears in every generation, every affected person has an affected parent and males and females are equally affected, it is likely autosomal dominant. If mostly males are affected, their mothers are unaffected carriers and there is no father-to-son transmission, it is likely X-linked recessive. If an affected mother passes the trait to all of her children while affected fathers pass it to none, it is mitochondrial. I would then assign genotypes to every individual, starting with affected individuals (aa or X^aY), and check that all parents and children are consistent.'},sources:[RULES,CAROLINA,OS_TRAITS,MP_INHERIT]};

export const G5={title:'Chromosomal inheritance, sex linkage and linkage maps',hook:'In 1910, Thomas Hunt Morgan found a single white-eyed male fruit fly among red-eyed siblings. Tracking that trait showed for the first time that a specific gene sits on a specific chromosome—the X. Chromosomal inheritance is 10% of the test, and its sample question uses the same kind of white-eye data.',objectives:['Explain the chromosome theory of inheritance and sex determination.','Solve X-linked crosses and interpret Drosophila eye-color data.','Explain gene linkage, recombination frequency and map units.','Interpret karyotypes and describe chromosomal mutations and aneuploidy.'],sections:[
['The chromosome theory and sex determination',[
'Walter **Sutton** and Theodor **Boveri** proposed the **chromosome theory of inheritance**: genes are located on chromosomes, and the behavior of chromosomes in meiosis explains Mendel’s laws. In humans and *Drosophila*, females are **XX** and males **XY**. The X chromosome carries many genes; the Y carries relatively few, including **SRY**, which triggers male development in mammals. Other systems exist: birds use ZW (females ZW), and some reptiles determine sex by egg temperature. In female mammals, one X in each cell is randomly inactivated early in development, forming a **Barr body**—which explains the patchy coat of calico cats, who are almost always female.',
match('Match the term to its meaning',[['Autosome','Any chromosome other than a sex chromosome'],['Sex chromosomes','X and Y in humans and fruit flies'],['SRY gene','Y-linked gene triggering male development'],['Barr body','Inactivated X chromosome in female cells'],['Hemizygous','Having one copy of an X-linked gene (males)'],['Chromosome theory','Genes are located on chromosomes']])]],
['X-linked inheritance in Drosophila',[
'Morgan crossed a **white-eyed male** with a **red-eyed female**. All F1 flies had red eyes, but in the F2 the white-eyed flies were **all males**. He concluded the eye-color gene is on the **X chromosome**: red (X^W) is dominant to white (X^w), and males, with one X, express whichever allele they carry. **Reciprocal crosses** reveal sex linkage because results differ depending on which parent carries the trait. In HOSA’s sample question, white-eyed females (X^wX^w) crossed with red-eyed males (X^WY) produce **all red-eyed daughters (X^WX^w)** and **all white-eyed sons (X^wY)**; crossing a red-eyed F1 female with a white-eyed male gives red and white flies in **equal numbers in both sexes**—the pattern of an **X-linked trait**.',
{t:'w',kind:'punnett',mode:'xlinked',title:'Build an X-linked cross',prompt:'Cross a white-eyed female with a red-eyed male, then cross the F1. Notice how the results differ between sons and daughters.'},
chk('White-eyed female fruit flies are crossed with red-eyed males. All daughters are red-eyed and all sons are white-eyed. What inheritance pattern does this show?',['X-linked trait','Simple autosomal dominance','Codominance','Incomplete dominance'],'Sons receive their only X from their mother.')]],
['Linked genes and recombination',[
'Genes close together on the same chromosome tend to be inherited together—they are **linked** and do not assort independently. In a dihybrid test cross of linked genes, **parental** combinations appear far more often than the 1:1:1:1 expected for independent assortment, and **recombinant** combinations appear only when **crossing over** occurs between the genes. The **recombination frequency** = recombinant offspring ÷ total offspring × 100. Alfred **Sturtevant**, Morgan’s student, realized that genes farther apart cross over more often, so recombination frequency can map genes: **1% recombination = 1 map unit (centimorgan, cM)**. Recombination frequencies never exceed about 50%, the value for unlinked genes. Skill IV asks competitors to distinguish inheritance from linked genes and genes on different chromosomes.',
{t:'example',title:'Calculate a map distance',prompt:'In a test cross of a fly heterozygous for body color and wing shape, offspring are: 880 gray normal and 860 black vestigial (parental), 140 gray vestigial and 120 black normal (recombinant). How far apart are the genes?',steps:['Total offspring = 880 + 860 + 140 + 120 = 2,000.','Recombinants = 140 + 120 = 260.','Recombination frequency = 260 ÷ 2,000 × 100 = 13%.'],answer:'About 13 map units (13 cM) apart. The large excess of parental types shows the genes are linked. (Counts are invented for practice.)'}]],
['Karyotypes and chromosome number',[
'A **karyotype** is an image of an individual’s chromosomes arranged in pairs by size, centromere position and banding pattern, usually from cells stopped in metaphase. Karyotypes reveal **aneuploidy**: **Down syndrome** (trisomy 21; 47,XX,+21 or 47,XY,+21), **Patau syndrome** (trisomy 13), **Edwards syndrome** (trisomy 18), **Turner syndrome** (45,X—female, short stature, usually infertile), **Klinefelter syndrome** (47,XXY—male, often tall, reduced fertility) and **triple X** (47,XXX). Most autosomal monosomies are not compatible with survival. **Polyploidy**—extra complete sets of chromosomes—is common in plants, such as wheat and strawberries, but not viable in humans.',
match('Match the karyotype to the condition',[['47,XY,+21','Down syndrome'],['45,X','Turner syndrome'],['47,XXY','Klinefelter syndrome'],['47,XX,+18','Edwards syndrome'],['47,XY,+13','Patau syndrome'],['Three complete chromosome sets (3n)','Triploidy']])]],
['Changes in chromosome structure',[
'Chromosome structure can change when chromosomes break and rejoin incorrectly. A **deletion** removes a segment (for example, cri-du-chat syndrome from a deletion on chromosome 5). A **duplication** repeats a segment. An **inversion** reverses a segment within a chromosome. A **translocation** moves a segment to a nonhomologous chromosome; a **reciprocal translocation** between chromosomes 9 and 22 creates the **Philadelphia chromosome** associated with chronic myeloid leukemia. Translocation carriers may be healthy but have a higher risk of chromosomally unbalanced children.',
sort('Structural change',['Deletion','Duplication','Inversion','Translocation'],[['A segment is lost',0],['A segment is copied twice',1],['A segment flips orientation',2],['A segment moves to a nonhomologous chromosome',3],['Cri-du-chat syndrome',0],['Philadelphia chromosome',3]])]],
["Ordering genes with map distances",["Map distances can be combined to find the **order** of genes on a chromosome. Because distances are roughly additive, the two genes with the **largest distance** are on the outside and the third lies between them. For example, if genes A and B are 10 map units apart, B and C are 4 map units apart and A and C are 14 map units apart, the order must be **A–B–C**, because 10 + 4 = 14. If A and C had been 6 units apart, the order would be **A–C–B** (6 + 4 = 10). Over long distances, double crossovers make measured recombination slightly lower than the true distance, which is why **three-point test crosses** are used to map genes more accurately.","Linkage maps were the first genetic maps; today, **physical maps** from DNA sequencing give exact base-pair positions, and genetic markers near disease genes help researchers find and test for those genes.",{"t":"check","q":"Genes R and S are 8 map units apart, S and T are 3 apart, and R and T are 5 apart. What is the gene order?","options":["R–T–S","R–S–T","S–R–T","T–R–S"],"why":"R–T (5) + T–S (3) = R–S (8), so T is between R and S."}]]
],takeaways:['Genes are on chromosomes (Sutton–Boveri); Morgan’s white-eyed flies showed X linkage—males are hemizygous and express their single X allele.','White-eyed females × red-eyed males give red daughters and white sons, the X-linked signature.','Linked genes give excess parental types; 1% recombination = 1 map unit; karyotypes reveal trisomies, Turner, Klinefelter and structural changes.'],terms:[['Linkage','Tendency of nearby genes on one chromosome to be inherited together.'],['Map unit (centimorgan)','Distance giving 1% recombination.'],['Karyotype','Ordered image of an individual’s chromosomes.']],mcqs:[q('White-eyed females crossed with red-eyed males give red-eyed daughters and white-eyed sons. The trait is:','X-linked','Codominant','Incompletely dominant','Simple autosomal dominant','A HOSA sample-style question.'),q('Who first showed that a specific gene is on a specific chromosome?','Thomas Hunt Morgan','Gregor Mendel','Rosalind Franklin','Charles Darwin','He used white-eyed Drosophila.'),q('A recombination frequency of 12% corresponds to:','12 map units','1.2 map units','120 map units','0.12 map units','1% = 1 cM.'),q('Linked genes in a test cross produce:','Many more parental types than recombinants','Equal numbers of all four types','Only recombinants','A 9:3:3:1 ratio','They tend to stay together.'),q('Turner syndrome has the karyotype:','45,X','47,XXY','47,XX,+21','46,XY','One X chromosome only.'),q('A Barr body is:','An inactivated X chromosome','A Y chromosome','An extra chromosome 21','A ribosome','X inactivation occurs in females.'),q('Moving a segment to a nonhomologous chromosome is a:','Translocation','Deletion','Inversion','Duplication','Example: the Philadelphia chromosome.'),q('Why are X-linked recessive traits more common in males?','Males have only one X, so one recessive allele is expressed','Males have two X chromosomes','The Y chromosome carries the dominant allele','Females cannot inherit them','Males are hemizygous.')],frq:{prompt:'A red-eyed female Drosophila is crossed with a white-eyed male, and all F1 flies are red-eyed. The F1 are crossed with each other. Predict the F2 by sex, explain the inheritance using chromosome notation, and describe how a reciprocal cross would differ.',rubric:[['Genotypes of parents and F1',['xw','x^w','x^wx^w','x^wy','carrier','red']],['F2 prediction by sex',['all females red','half the males','white-eyed males','3:1','males']],['X-linked explanation',['x chromosome','hemizygous','one x','mother','sons']],['Reciprocal cross',['reciprocal','white-eyed female','red-eyed male','all sons white','daughters red']]],model:'The red-eyed female is X^WX^W and the white-eyed male is X^wY. All F1 daughters are X^WX^w (red-eyed carriers) and all F1 sons are X^WY (red-eyed). Crossing the F1 (X^WX^w × X^WY) gives daughters X^WX^W and X^WX^w—all red-eyed—and sons X^WY (red) and X^wY (white) in equal numbers. Overall the F2 is 3 red : 1 white, but every white-eyed fly is male, because males are hemizygous: they inherit their single X from their mother and express whichever allele it carries. In the reciprocal cross, a white-eyed female (X^wX^w) with a red-eyed male (X^WY) produces all red-eyed daughters (X^WX^w) and all white-eyed sons (X^wY) in the F1, a result that differs from the first cross and reveals that the gene is X-linked.'},sources:[RULES,CAROLINA,OS_CHROM,OS_DISORD]};
